<DataShopLayout current="about" title="People | DATA" description="Jai Bhagat, founder of Dharmic Data, and Imtiaz, advisor to DATA.">
  <section class="page-hero people-hero">
    <p class="overline">The people behind the imprint</p>
    <h1>Built from a life<br />of translation.</h1>
    <p>Between software and teaching, private memory and public systems, Queens and everywhere the work travels.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="people-grid">
    <article class="person-card founder">
      <div class="portrait monogram">JB</div>
      <div>
        <p class="overline">Founder / Dharmic Data</p>
        <h2>Jai Bhagat</h2>
        <p>Jai moved through investment management and technology sales before learning to code, then turned that transition into a practice of teaching other people how to make difficult systems legible.</p>
        <p>Across more than a decade in software and teaching—including developer education—his work has kept returning to the same question: how do you turn information into a next move a real person can use?</p>
        <p>Dharmic Data is the answer in progress. DATA is its cultural imprint: a place where maps, archives, software, and textiles can carry the same evidence-led method.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="person-card advisor">
      <div class="portrait monogram">I</div>
      <div>
        <p class="overline">Advisor / DATA</p>
        <h2>Imtiaz</h2>
        <p>Imtiaz advises DATA as the imprint develops—helping the work hold onto context, restraint, and accountability while it moves from a private reference to a public object.</p>
        <p>The role is intentionally practical: ask what the artifact communicates, what it leaves out, and whether the next edition earns its place.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="arc">
    <p class="overline">Founder arc</p>
    <div class="arc-track">
      <article><span>01</span><h2>Sell</h2><p>Learn what people say they need—and what the numbers reveal instead.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><h2>Build</h2><p>Learn software as a material for turning decisions into working systems.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><h2>Teach</h2><p>Make the system understandable enough for someone else to use and question.</p></article>
      <article><span>04</span><h2>Archive</h2><p>Preserve cultural memory as a navigable object, not a disappearing feed.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>
</DataShopLayout>
